From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christophe Pettus <xof@thebuild.com>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 21:15:17 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 10/22] H9: make lazy xref repair thread-safe; add
 Pdf::is_encrypted

The first lookup that finds no object where the xref table says one is
rebuilds the table by scanning the whole file. That path was not safe
to reach from several threads at once: `XRef::get_with` took the map
lock with `try_read().unwrap()`, `repair()` with `try_write().unwrap()`
followed by `assert!(!repaired)`, and the scan ran while the write lock
was held. Two threads looking up objects of one document while either
of them triggered a repair could therefore panic, and every other lookup
was locked out for the duration of the scan.

Now `get_with` takes a blocking read lock. `repair()` scans the file
bytes, which are immutable, without holding any lock, then takes a
blocking write lock, re-checks `repaired` and returns if another thread
got there first; otherwise it swaps the new map in, sets the flag and
frees the old map after releasing the lock. Concurrent repairs are
idempotent, the map is replaced at most once, and other lookups block
only for the swap.

`get_with` also reads the `repaired` flag under the same lock as the
entry it looks up, instead of calling `is_repaired()` after the read
failed. Otherwise a thread holding a stale entry from the old map, whose
read failed just after another thread swapped the repaired map in, saw
`repaired == true`, concluded that the repaired table was wrong too and
returned `None` for an object that exists. Lock poisoning is ignored: the writer only moves two
fields, so the data cannot be left half-updated.

`xref::repair_hooks` (hidden, per thread) counts repair scans and swaps
and can run a callback between the scan and the swap, so tests can
force several threads into the repair at once.

Also add `Pdf::is_encrypted()`, which reports whether the document was
opened with a decryptor installed. Callers previously had to infer it,
for example by opening a second time with a wrong password.
